Chapter 2: Types of Networks
What Are Types of Networks?

A network type is a way of classifying a network based on how large the area is and what it is used for.

Think about transportation:

Walking is for short distances.
A car is for city travel.
A train is for longer distances.
An airplane is for international travel.

Similarly, different network types are designed for different coverage areas.

The most common types are:

PAN – Personal Area Network
LAN – Local Area Network
CAN – Campus Area Network
MAN – Metropolitan Area Network
WAN – Wide Area Network
SAN – Storage Area Network
1. PAN (Personal Area Network)

A Personal Area Network (PAN) connects devices belonging to one person over a very short distance, usually within 10 meters.

Examples
Phone connected to Bluetooth earbuds.
Smartwatch connected to a phone.
Laptop connected to a wireless mouse.
Bluetooth keyboard connected to a tablet.
Real-Life Example

You're listening to music:

Your phone stores the song.
The song is sent via Bluetooth.
Your earbuds receive and play it.

This creates a PAN.

Technologies Used
Bluetooth
NFC (Near Field Communication)
USB
Infrared (older technology)
Advantages
Easy to set up.
Low power consumption.
Inexpensive.
Disadvantages
Very short range.
Supports only a few devices.
Lower speed than larger networks.
Cybersecurity Note

Bluetooth devices can be vulnerable if left discoverable. Keeping Bluetooth off when not needed reduces unnecessary exposure.

2. LAN (Local Area Network)

A Local Area Network (LAN) connects devices within a small geographic area, such as a home, school, office, or computer lab.

Examples
Home Wi-Fi.
College computer lab.
Office network.
School network.
Real-Life Example

Imagine your college lab has:

40 computers
2 printers
1 server
1 Internet connection

All devices are connected through switches and a router, allowing file sharing, printing, and Internet access.

This is a LAN.

Technologies Used
Ethernet cables
Wi-Fi
Switches
Routers
Advantages
High speed.
Easy resource sharing.
Lower cost than larger networks.
Easier management.
Disadvantages
Limited coverage area.
Failure of key devices (like a switch) can affect many users.
Cybersecurity Note

Most organizations implement security controls inside LANs, such as VLANs, access controls, endpoint protection, and internal firewalls.

3. CAN (Campus Area Network)

A Campus Area Network (CAN) connects multiple LANs within a campus or organization.

Examples
University campus.
Large hospital.
Corporate campus.
Research center.
Real-Life Example

A university has:

Engineering building (LAN)
Library (LAN)
Administration building (LAN)
Hostel (LAN)

All these LANs are interconnected, forming a CAN.

Advantages
Centralized management.
High-speed communication across the campus.
Efficient sharing of servers and services.
Disadvantages
Higher installation cost.
Requires skilled administrators.
Cybersecurity Note

Universities often separate student, faculty, guest, and administrative traffic using VLANs and network segmentation.

4. MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)

A Metropolitan Area Network (MAN) covers a city or large metropolitan region.

Examples
A city's cable TV network.
Municipal government network.
Bank branches across a city.
Real-Life Example

A bank has 25 branches across Delhi. Each branch has its own LAN, and all branches are connected using leased lines or fiber optics.

Together, they form a MAN.

Advantages
Covers larger areas than LAN or CAN.
High-speed communication between locations.
Disadvantages
More expensive to build and maintain.
Requires advanced infrastructure.
Cybersecurity Note

Traffic between branches is often encrypted using VPNs to protect sensitive financial information.

5. WAN (Wide Area Network)

A Wide Area Network (WAN) connects networks over countries or continents.

The Internet is the largest WAN in the world.
Examples
Internet.
Global company networks.
International bank networks.
Cloud services.
Real-Life Example

Suppose a company has offices in:

Delhi
Mumbai
London
New York

Each office has its own LAN, and all offices are securely connected over the Internet or private leased links.

This forms a WAN.

Advantages
Global communication.
Centralized access to applications and data.
Supports remote work.
Disadvantages
Higher latency than LAN.
More expensive.
More complex to manage.
Cybersecurity Note

WAN security often includes VPNs, firewalls, intrusion detection systems (IDS), intrusion prevention systems (IPS), and continuous monitoring through SOC teams.

6. SAN (Storage Area Network)

A Storage Area Network (SAN) is a specialized high-speed network that connects servers to shared storage devices.

Where Is It Used?
Data centers.
Cloud providers.
Enterprise organizations.
Benefits
Fast access to storage.
Centralized data management.
High availability.
Cybersecurity Note

Since SANs often store critical business data, organizations use strong access controls, encryption, and backup strategies to protect them.

Comparison Table
